        <span>•</span>
        <span>Trade Installation Guide</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-3 tracking-tight">
        8-Foot vs. 12-Foot Drywall Sheets: Which Should You Choose?
      </h1>
      <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
        While 8-foot drywall sheets are easier to carry, they create 50% more seams to tape and sand. Professional drywallers almost always hang 12-foot sheets. Here is how sheet sizing affects your joints, labor, and budget.
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Interactive Tool Callout -->
    <div class="p-6 bg-gradient-to-br from-blue-50 to-indigo-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl mb-8 shadow-xs flex flex-col sm:flex-row items-start sm:items-center justify-between gap-4">
      <div>
        <h2 class="text-base font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1">Calculate drywall sheets for your room dimensions</h2>
        <p class="text-xs text-gray-600">Toggle between 4x8, 4x10, and 4x12 sheets to see exact sheet counts and mud buckets needed.</p>
      </div>
      <a href="/calculators/drywall" class="shrink-0 inline-flex items-center gap-2 bg-blue-600 hover:bg-blue-700 text-white text-xs font-bold px-4 py-2.5 rounded-lg transition-colors shadow-2xs">
        <span>Open Drywall Calculator</span>
        <span>→</span>
      </a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 space-y-6">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">1. Tapered Factory Edges vs. Butt Joints</h2>
      <p>
        To understand why sheet size matters, you must understand how drywall is manufactured:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Tapered Factory Edges:</strong> The long 8-foot or 12-foot edges are recessed (tapered down about 1/16"). When two tapered edges meet horizontally, they create a slight depression that easily accepts paper tape and joint compound without creating a bulge on the flat wall.</li>
        <li><strong>Butt Joints:</strong> The short 4-foot ends of drywall are square-cut with zero taper. When two butt ends meet, tape and mud sit directly on top of the drywall surface. To hide a butt joint, you must feather mud out 18 to 24 inches wide on both sides.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">2. Real-World Seam Math on a 12-Foot Wall</h2>
      <p>
        Consider a standard 12-foot long bedroom wall with an 8-foot ceiling:
      </p>

      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 not-prose mb-6">
        <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 shadow-xs">
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-amber-700 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">USING 4&times;8 SHEETS</div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Requires 3 Sheets</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            You must hang two full sheets and cut a third. This creates <strong>two vertical butt joints</strong> running from floor to ceiling (8 linear feet of difficult-to-feather butt seams) plus the center horizontal seam.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="bg-white p-5 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 shadow-xs">
          <div class="text-xs font-bold text-emerald-700 uppercase tracking-wider mb-1">USING 4&times;12 SHEETS</div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Requires 2 Sheets</h3>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-600 leading-relaxed">
            Two 12-foot sheets span the wall from corner to corner horizontally. Result: <strong>ZERO vertical butt joints</strong>. You only have one continuous tapered horizontal seam to tape!
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">3. Weight &amp; Maneuverability Comparison</h2>
      <p>
        The main drawback of 12-foot drywall is physical weight:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>4&times;8 Sheet (1/2" Regular):</strong> Weighs ~48 to 52 lbs. Easily handled by a single capable DIYer; fits into tight basements and around stair corners.</li>
        <li><strong>4&times;12 Sheet (1/2" Regular):</strong> Weighs ~75 to 80 lbs. Extremely awkward to carry solo; requires two people or a drywall panel lift. Difficult to maneuver through 90-degree hallway corners.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">4. Recommendations for DIY Homeowners</h2>
      <p>
        Use this rule of thumb for your next renovation:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Main Floor &amp; Open Basements:</strong> Use <strong>12-foot sheets</strong> horizontally on walls and ceilings to cut taping labor by 35%.</li>
        <li><strong>Small Bathrooms, Closets &amp; Upper Floors with Tight Stairs:</strong> Use <strong>8-foot sheets</strong> to avoid damaged corners and broken sheets during transit.</li>
        <li><strong>9-Foot Ceilings:</strong> Order <strong>54-inch wide drywall (4.5 &times; 12 ft)</strong> so two horizontal sheets cover the entire 108" wall height without narrow 12" filler strips.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

